{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"iterators","title":"Iterators & Iterables","group":"Collections & Iteration","order":37,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The iteration protocols are what make <code>for…of</code>, spread, destructuring, <code>Array.from</code> and <code>Promise.all</code> work on any collection — including your own.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>The two protocols</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Iterable</h3><p>An object with a <code>[Symbol.iterator]()</code> method that returns an iterator. Arrays, strings, Maps, Sets, <code>arguments</code>, NodeLists and generators are iterable. Plain objects are <strong>not</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-track-next\"></i>Iterator</h3><p>An object with a <code>next()</code> method returning <code>{ value, done }</code>. Optional <code>return()</code> is called on early exit (<code>break</code>).</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const arr = [\"a\", \"b\"];\nconst it = arr[Symbol.iterator]();\nconsole.log(it.next(), it.next(), it.next());\ntry {\n  for (const x of { a: 1 }) {}\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.message);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{ value: 'a', done: false } { value: 'b', done: false } { value: undefined, done: true }\n{(intermediate value)} is not iterable</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>A custom iterable</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Range {\n  constructor(start, end, step = 1) { Object.assign(this, { start, end, step }); }\n  [Symbol.iterator]() {\n    let current = this.start;\n    const { end, step } = this;\n    return {\n      next: () =&gt;\n        current &lt;= end ? { value: (current += step) - step, done: false } : { value: undefined, done: true },\n      [Symbol.iterator]() { return this; },   // iterators are usually iterable too\n    };\n  }\n}\nconst r = new Range(1, 10, 3);\nconsole.log([...r]);\nconst [first, second] = r;\nconsole.log(first, second, Math.max(...r), Array.from(r, (n) =&gt; n * 10));\nfor (const n of new Range(1, 3)) console.log(n);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 1, 4, 7, 10 ]\n1 4 10 [ 10, 40, 70, 100 ]\n1\n2\n3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hourglass\"></i>Iterators are lazy and one-shot</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const it = [1, 2, 3].values();\nconsole.log([...it], [...it]);          // second spread: already exhausted\nconst m = new Map([[\"x\", 1], [\"y\", 2]]);\nconsole.log(m.entries().next().value);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 1, 2, 3 ] []\n[ 'x', 1 ]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i>Iterator helpers (ES2025, Node 22+)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function* naturals() { let n = 1; while (true) yield n++; }   // infinite!\nconst firstSquaresOfEvens = naturals()\n  .filter((n) =&gt; n % 2 === 0)\n  .map((n) =&gt; n * n)\n  .take(4)\n  .toArray();\nconsole.log(firstSquaresOfEvens);\nconsole.log(Iterator.from([5, 6, 7]).drop(1).reduce((a, b) =&gt; a + b, 0));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 4, 16, 36, 64 ]\n13</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Iterator helpers are <strong>lazy</strong>: nothing is computed until <code>toArray()</code>, <code>for…of</code> or a reducer pulls values — so they work on infinite sequences where array methods cannot.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you make an object iterable?</summary><div><p>Give it a <code>[Symbol.iterator]</code> method that returns an object with <code>next()</code> returning <code>{ value, done }</code> — or, easiest, make <code>[Symbol.iterator]</code> a generator: <code>*[Symbol.iterator]() { yield* this.items; }</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}